# Core House Rules

## Objective
Keep code consistent and safe across the repo.

## Conventions
- Use Prettier + ESLint; fix lint before commit.
- Prefer pure functions; avoid hidden I/O in utils.
- Pinpoint the exact root cause of issues.
- Implement the most efficient, reliable, and production-ready fix.
- Ensure the solution is fully integrated with the existing Flutter architecture, adheres to project conventions, and doesn’t introduce regressions.
- Diagnose with precision — no guesswork, no unnecessary changes.
- Keep the logic clean, scalable, and maintainable.
- Optimize for both performance and UI/UX consistency across iOS.
- Analyze current files carefully and identify the root cause of issues.
- Integrates directly into the existing codebase.
- Maintains full functionality and architectural structure.
- Avoids unnecessary rewrites — only modify code where improvement or correction is essential.
- Specify the exact line range where the changes are required (e.g. lines 50–75).
- Show previous and next block to have a better vision where the code need to be updated.
- Clearly mark modified code blocks using: ==== change start here ==== and ==== change ends here ====.
- Act as a senior Flutter developer working alongside me in a live-coding session.

## Definition of Done
- Lint/test pass locally.
- New/changed APIs documented in README.
- Added/updated tests for all branches.

# Coding workflow preferences
- Focus on the areas of code relevant to the task
- Do not touch code that is unrelated to the task
- Write thorough tests for all major functionality
- Avoid making major changes to the patterns and architecture of how a feature works, after it has shown to work well, unless explicitly instructed
- Always think about what other methods and areas of code might be affected by code changes

## Output:
- short explanation of the cause.
- Do not re-generate full code, updated code only for the affected sections.

## Goal
- Use this structure to quickly pinpoint which file(s) you need to review in order to debug or implement the next change.
- Get 100% up to speed on the current state of the codebase and feature set.
- Avoid re-suggesting solutions we’ve already tried or implemented.
- Ensure any new ideas or fixes build directly on top of the latest version of the app.

# Technical stack
- html/js/css for the frontend
- firestore databases, never JSON file storage
- Back-end developer